"""
The frame algebra shared by every attitude and airspeed reference.

This holds the part of a sensor that is physics rather than instrumentation:
reading the aircraft datum frame and the air mass, and reducing them to pitch,
bank and true airspeed. What a particular unit then does with those three
numbers --- report them exactly, or sample, bias, quantise and corrupt them
--- is left to the component that extends this one.

Splitting the sensing out from the controller is not cosmetic. A digital
autopilot is a signal processor: it cannot reach into a multibody frame, it
reads whatever the sensors hand it. Keeping the frame algebra on this side
leaves the controller a pure signal block, which is both how the hardware is
arranged and what makes a sampled implementation possible at all.

# Reading attitude from the frame

Column 2 of `frame_a.R` is the world vertical resolved in body axes, which
for the yaw-pitch-roll sequence of this frame convention is

    [sin(theta), cos(theta)*cos(phi), -cos(theta)*sin(phi)]

so pitch and bank come straight out of it. This is exact at any heading and
singular only with the nose straight up.
"""
partial component AttitudeSensorBase
  "Mount on the aircraft datum"
  frame_a = Frame3D() {^frame_a}
  "Density and wind, so the airspeed is true airspeed and not ground speed"
  air = AirStateInput() {^air}
  "Reported pitch attitude, positive nose up"
  theta = RealOutput() {^theta}
  "Reported bank angle, positive right wing down"
  phi = RealOutput() {^phi}
  "Reported airspeed"
  V = RealOutput() {^V}
  "Airspeed regularisation, so the speed stays differentiable at rest"
  parameter v_eps::Velocity = 0.5
  "Velocity of the datum relative to the air mass, world frame"
  variable v_rel_0::Velocity[3]
  "Pitch attitude as the frame actually holds it, before any sensor error"
  variable theta_true::Real
  "Bank angle as the frame actually holds it, before any sensor error"
  variable phi_true::Real
  "True airspeed as the air bus actually gives it, before any sensor error"
  variable V_true::Real
relations
  # A sensor applies nothing to the frame it reads
  frame_a.f = [0, 0, 0]
  frame_a.tau = [0, 0, 0]
  v_rel_0 = der(frame_a.r_0) - [air.v_wind_x, air.v_wind_y, air.v_wind_z]
  V_true = sqrt(v_rel_0[1] ^ 2 + v_rel_0[2] ^ 2 + v_rel_0[3] ^ 2 + v_eps ^ 2)
  theta_true = atan(frame_a.R[1, 2], sqrt(frame_a.R[2, 2] ^ 2 + frame_a.R[3, 2] ^ 2))
  phi_true = atan(-frame_a.R[3, 2], frame_a.R[2, 2])
metadata {
  "_links": {
    "frame_a": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 300, "x2": 50, "y2": 400}}}},
    "air": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 600, "x2": 50, "y2": 700}}}},
    "theta": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 200, "x2": 1050, "y2": 300}}}},
    "phi": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 450, "x2": 1050, "y2": 550}}}},
    "V": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 700, "x2": 1050, "y2": 800}}}}
  }
}
end

"""
Attitude and airspeed reference: the sensing half of an autopilot.

The ideal unit. It reports exactly what the frame and the air bus carry ---
no bias, no noise, no quantisation, no lag, no sample rate. Use it when the
question is about the airframe or the control law and sensor error would only
get in the way.

`NoisyAttitudeSensor` is the same instrument with the error terms present,
and is a drop-in replacement for this one.
"""
component AttitudeSensor
  extends AttitudeSensorBase
relations
  theta = theta_true
  phi = phi_true
  V = V_true
metadata {"Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://Multtest/AttitudeSensor.svg"}}}
end

"""
One measurement channel of an instrument: sample, bias, noise, quantise.

This is a local replacement for `DiscreteComponents.SampleWithADEffects`,
which does the same four things in the same order. It exists because the
stock block gives no way to make two channels independent, and two channels
of one instrument that share a common-mode error are not a noise model ---
they are a disturbance, and a fusion or multi-axis loop will treat them as
one.

# Why a seed is not enough

The library's noise source computes `seeded_randn(seed, t)`, which builds a
`StableRNG` from `hash(t, hash(seed))` and takes a single draw. A single draw
from a freshly seeded linear generator is close to a linear function of its
state, so two channels evaluated at the *same* `t` with different seeds come
out correlated --- measurably, not theoretically. Over a 40 s run at 100 Hz:

    seeds (1,2,3)                 worst pairwise |corr| = 0.56
    seeds (1,97,199)              worst pairwise |corr| = 0.50
    best triple searched in 1:400 worst pairwise |corr| = 0.17
    fraction of all pairs in 1:400 with |corr| > 0.05:  0.92

against a sampling floor of 0.016. There is no seed choice that fixes it.

# What does work

Hold the seed and move the *time* the generator is keyed on. Distinct `t`
values hash to unrelated states, and the correlation drops to the sampling
floor (measured 0.016 to 0.033 across sample rates of 5, 10 and 20 ms). So
each channel takes one `seed` --- the seed of the whole instrument, which is
what makes a run reproducible --- and its own `t_offset`.

`t_offset` must be distinct per channel and incommensurate with the sample
period: an offset that is an exact multiple of `Ts` just shifts one channel
onto another channel's samples, which is perfect correlation at a lag rather
than independence. Irrational-looking constants are the simple way to be
sure.

# Order of operations

Sample, then bias and noise, then quantise --- the order the hardware
imposes, since an ADC quantises whatever analogue signal reaches it, errors
included. Quantising last is what puts the output exactly on the quantiser
grid, which is a property worth keeping if anything downstream is counting
levels.
"""
component NoisyChannel@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "The continuous quantity being measured"
  u = RealInput() {^u}
  "The reading, on the clock"
  y = RealOutput@[clk]() {^y}
  "Samples the measured quantity onto the clock"
  s_u = DiscreteComponents.Sampler() {^s_u}
  "Simulation time, which is what the noise is keyed on"
  t_src = BlockComponents.Sources.ContinuousClock() {^t_src}
  "Samples time onto the same clock"
  s_t = DiscreteComponents.Sampler() {^s_t}
  "Clips to full scale and quantises, as an ADC does"
  q = DiscreteComponents.Quantization(bits = bits, quantized = quantized, y_min = y_min, y_max = y_max) {^q}
  "Standing offset of this channel"
  parameter bias::Real = 0.0
  "Noise standard deviation of this channel"
  parameter sigma::Real = 0.0
  "RNG seed; shared with the rest of the instrument"
  parameter seed::Integer = 1
  "Time offset that makes this channel independent of its neighbours"
  parameter t_offset::Real = 0.0
  "Bottom of full scale; the quantiser clips here"
  parameter y_min::Real = -1.0
  "Top of full scale; the quantiser clips here"
  parameter y_max::Real = 1.0
  "Quantiser word length"
  parameter bits::Integer = 16
  "Whether the reading is quantised at all"
  parameter quantized::Boolean = true
  "Sampled simulation time"
  variable td::Real
relations
  td@clk = s_t.y@clk
  q.u@clk = s_u.y@clk + bias + sigma * DiscreteComponents.seeded_randn(seed, td + t_offset)
  connect(u, s_u.u) {^id7}
  connect(t_src.y, s_t.u) {^id8}
  connect(q.y, y) {^id9}
metadata {
  "_links": {
    "u": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 450, "x2": 50, "y2": 550}}}},
    "y": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 450, "x2": 1050, "y2": 550}}}},
    "s_u": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 200, "y1": 420, "x2": 300, "y2": 520}}}
    },
    "t_src": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 40, "y1": 760, "x2": 140, "y2": 860}}}
    },
    "s_t": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 200, "y1": 760, "x2": 300, "y2": 860}}}
    },
    "q": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 620, "y1": 420, "x2": 720, "y2": 520}}}
    },
    "id7": {},
    "id8": {},
    "id9": {}
  }
}
end

"""
The same attitude and airspeed reference with the instrument errors modelled.

A drop-in replacement for `AttitudeSensor`: same ports, same frame algebra,
and still a continuous-signal interface, so a harness swaps one for the other
and changes nothing else. What it adds is everything between the physical
quantity and the number the flight computer reads:

* **A sample rate of its own.** The unit runs on its own `PeriodicClock` at
  `Ts`, independent of the autopilot's clock. This is how the hardware is
  arranged --- an AHRS and a flight computer are separate boxes on separate
  oscillators --- and it means the autopilot sees a signal that is already
  held, so any aliasing between the two rates is in the model rather than
  assumed away.
* **A fixed bias per channel.** A standing offset is the error that a control
  loop cannot reject, because the integrator trims the aircraft to the wrong
  attitude rather than trimming the error away. `bias_theta` is the one to
  reach for when the question is how much a mis-levelled unit costs.
* **Gaussian noise per channel**, drawn so that the three channels really are
  independent --- see `NoisyChannel`, which explains why that takes more than
  giving each channel its own seed. One `seed` makes the whole run
  reproducible.
* **Quantisation**, over a declared full-scale range. This is the error that
  does not shrink when the aircraft flies more smoothly, and the one that
  sets the floor on a rate term computed by differencing.

# Choosing the ranges

The quantiser clips at `y_min`/`y_max`, so the ranges are full-scale limits
and not a formality: pitch beyond `theta_range` reads as `theta_range`. The
defaults cover an aircraft that is the right way up and inside its envelope
(+/- 90 deg pitch, +/- 180 deg bank, 0 to 80 m/s) at 16 bits, which puts the
quantisation step at about 5e-5 rad and 1.2e-3 m/s --- an order below the
default noise, so noise dominates, as it does in a decent unit. Dropping
`bits` is how to make quantisation the dominant error instead.

# Note on the rate terms downstream

`DigitalAutopilot` forms its rate terms by differencing successive samples,
so measurement noise of standard deviation `sigma` reaches the rate term
multiplied by `sqrt(2)/Ts_autopilot`. At the defaults that is a rate noise of
about 0.14 rad/s rms on a gain of 0.06, or 0.009 rad of elevator --- small,
but it is the term that will bite first if the noise is raised.
"""
component NoisyAttitudeSensor
  extends AttitudeSensorBase
  "Pins the unit's sample rate"
  clock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = Ts) {^clock}
  # The three channels never meet in an equation, so they reach the compiler
  # as three separate clock partitions, and a clock source pins only the
  # partition its own signal is in --- the other two arrive as unresolved
  # `InferredDiscrete` and the build fails. A second and third `PeriodicClock`
  # is not the fix: two clock sources in one component body is an
  # `invalid-clock-usage` error. `ClockPropagator` is the block for this. It
  # ties two signals' clocks without tying their values, which is exactly the
  # statement that all three channels run off the one oscillator.
  "Ties the bank channel to the pitch channel's clock"
  tie_phi = DiscreteComponents.ClockPropagator() {^tie_phi}
  "Ties the airspeed channel to the pitch channel's clock"
  tie_V = DiscreteComponents.ClockPropagator() {^tie_V}
  "Sample, bias, add noise, quantise --- pitch"
  ch_theta = NoisyChannel(bias = bias_theta, sigma = sigma_theta, seed = seed, t_offset = t_offset_theta, bits = bits, quantized = quantized, y_min = -theta_range, y_max = theta_range) {^ch_theta}
  "Sample, bias, add noise, quantise --- bank"
  ch_phi = NoisyChannel(bias = bias_phi, sigma = sigma_phi, seed = seed, t_offset = t_offset_phi, bits = bits, quantized = quantized, y_min = -phi_range, y_max = phi_range) {^ch_phi}
  "Sample, bias, add noise, quantise --- airspeed"
  ch_V = NoisyChannel(bias = bias_V, sigma = sigma_V, seed = seed, t_offset = t_offset_V, bits = bits, quantized = quantized, y_min = 0.0, y_max = V_range) {^ch_V}
  "Hold back to continuous time, so the unit is a drop-in for the ideal one"
  h_theta = DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold() {^h_theta}
  h_phi = DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold() {^h_phi}
  h_V = DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold() {^h_V}
  "Sample period of the unit; the clock is built with the component, so this is structural"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.01
  "Standing pitch offset, the error an attitude loop cannot reject"
  parameter bias_theta::Angle = 0.0
  "Standing bank offset"
  parameter bias_phi::Angle = 0.0
  "Standing airspeed offset, e.g. a static source error"
  parameter bias_V::Velocity = 0.0
  "Pitch noise, standard deviation"
  parameter sigma_theta::Angle = 0.002
  "Bank noise, standard deviation"
  parameter sigma_phi::Angle = 0.002
  "Airspeed noise, standard deviation"
  parameter sigma_V::Velocity = 0.3
  "Pitch full scale; the quantiser clips here"
  parameter theta_range::Angle = 1.5708
  "Bank full scale; the quantiser clips here"
  parameter phi_range::Angle = 3.1416
  "Airspeed full scale; the quantiser clips here"
  parameter V_range::Velocity = 80.0
  "Whether the reported values are quantised at all"
  parameter quantized::Boolean = true
  "Quantiser word length, all three channels"
  parameter bits::Integer = 16
  "RNG seed for the whole unit; the same seed replays the same run exactly"
  parameter seed::Integer = 1
  """
  Time offsets that decorrelate the three channels. They must stay distinct
  and incommensurate with `Ts`; `NoisyChannel` explains why the decorrelation
  is done this way rather than with three seeds.
  """
  parameter t_offset_theta::Real = 0.0
  parameter t_offset_phi::Real = 0.57721566
  parameter t_offset_V::Real = 2.71828183
relations
  ch_theta.u = theta_true
  ch_phi.u = phi_true
  ch_V.u = V_true
  connect(ch_theta.y, h_theta.u, clock.y, tie_phi.u, tie_V.u) {^id10}
  connect(ch_phi.y, h_phi.u, tie_phi.y) {^id11}
  connect(ch_V.y, h_V.u, tie_V.y) {^id12}
  connect(h_theta.y, theta) {^id13}
  connect(h_phi.y, phi) {^id14}
  connect(h_V.y, V) {^id15}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {
    "icons": {"default": "dyad://Multtest/AttitudeSensor.svg"},
    "labels": [
      {"label": "$(instance)", "x": 500, "y": 1100, "rot": 0},
      {
        "label": "Ts = $(Ts)",
        "x": 500,
        "y": 1230,
        "rot": 0,
        "attrs": {"font-size": "90", "fill": "#6b7a88"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "_links": {
    "clock": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 440, "y1": 20, "x2": 540, "y2": 120}}}
    },
    "tie_phi": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 880, "y1": 280, "x2": 980, "y2": 380}}}
    },
    "tie_V": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 880, "y1": 580, "x2": 980, "y2": 680}}}
    },
    "ch_theta": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 300, "y1": 120, "x2": 400, "y2": 220}}}
    },
    "ch_phi": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 300, "y1": 420, "x2": 400, "y2": 520}}}
    },
    "ch_V": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 300, "y1": 720, "x2": 400, "y2": 820}}}
    },
    "h_theta": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 620, "y1": 120, "x2": 720, "y2": 220}}}
    },
